`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"One MinHash entry agrees with probability equal to the Jaccard similarity",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Near-duplicate detection needs a similarity that a short, fixed-size summary of each document can estimate. The first step turns a page into a set. In the exercise, the text is first normalized to Unicode NFC and lowercased. NFC gives each character a single encoding, so an accented letter stored as one code point matches the same letter stored as a base letter plus a combining accent. Lowercasing makes "The" and "the" the same word. A window of five consecutive words then slides along the text, and each position of the window gives one shingle. The page becomes the set of its word 5-grams. Every similarity value on this tab refers to the Jaccard similarity of Formula (1). It is 1 for identical sets and 0 for disjoint ones.'}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Comparing full shingle sets for every pair of pages costs far too much for a corpus, so MinHash summarizes each set with a random ranking of shingles."}),e.jsxs(t.h4,{children:["Why one entry agrees with probability ",e.jsx(i,{math:"J",display:!1})]}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
