`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"The threshold decides. The banding target only aims."}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Exact verification and a fixed keep-one rule make removal reproducible",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A band collision is a reason to compare two documents, and the comparison makes the decision. For every candidate pair, compute the exact Jaccard similarity of the two full shingle sets and compare it with a verification threshold. Candidate generation can afford false positives, because this check rejects them. The course pins no verification threshold. It is a parameter of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"CurationConfig"}),", the exercise's configuration dataclass, which holds every parameter and seed of your curation pipeline. You choose the threshold and record it there. The widget starts it at 0.75, the same number as FineWeb's target, here used as a cutoff on the exact Jaccard of a proposed pair."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Verified pairs are then grouped into duplicate clusters. Treat each document as a node and each verified pair as an edge. A duplicate cluster is a connected component of that graph. If A is verified against B, and B against C, then A, B and C form one cluster, even if A and C were never verified as a pair."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each cluster then keeps one document under an explicit keep-one rule. The rule ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"first"})," keeps the document crawled earliest, and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"newest"})," keeps the one crawled latest. On equal dates the document earlier in the input wins, so every cluster keeps exactly one document. The rule ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"newest"})," can favor a later revision of a page. Retention is a provenance policy, separate from the similarity test."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The reason to prefer this deterministic form is reproducibility. With the verification threshold and the keep-one rule recorded, the same corpus always yields the same survivors. A random keep would yield a different survivor on every run, and a removal you cannot re-derive is a removal you cannot audit."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["This is the rule the exercise implements, and the widget demonstrates it. FineWeb, the source of the 14 × 8 banding, works differently. It treats a shared band as sufficient evidence and removes the documents that collide in any band. It keeps one randomly chosen member of each duplicate cluster, and its published description names no exact-Jaccard verification stage ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2024fineweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2024"}),". The published recipe closest to the exercise's rule is The Stack v3's code pipeline, which verifies candidates exactly and then chooses the survivor by a fixed provenance ordering ",e.jsx(s,{id:"lozhkov2026stackv3",children:"Lozhkov et al., 2026"}),'. The optional slide "Code runs near-deduplication at its own settings" describes that pipeline.']}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The widget shows one candidate pair and its exact Jaccard. A threshold above that value leaves the pair unverified, and no keep-one rule applies. While the pair is verified, the keep-one buttons choose between Document A, the older copy, and Document B, the newer one."})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Per-snapshot versus global deduplication",accent:2,number:"16",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Snapshot: one crawl of the web, one date"}),`
