`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Decontamination protects scores. PII filtering protects people."}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A marked cell claims a check ran, and an empty cell claims nothing",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A public benchmark item is text like any other. It can reach a training corpus wherever data enters a pipeline: with the crawl, in synthetic data, and in the data of later training stages. Decontamination removes training text that overlaps an evaluation benchmark, so that a score measures the model on items it has not seen. Most of Week 7's countermeasures to contamination act on the evaluation. Decontamination acts mainly on the training data, so the question is at which stages to run it."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The map orders a pipeline's stages from acquisition to evaluation. Its two lanes remove text for different reasons: evaluation decontamination, and the filtering of personally identifiable information (PII). Each marker records one check that one report documents at one stage. A marked cell claims that the report states the check ran there. An empty cell claims nothing, because reports often leave out routine stages. Two recipes therefore compare on a stage only when both reports address it."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["GPT-3 filtered text that overlapped its benchmarks out of its collected training corpus, which puts its first marker in the Filtering column. A bug left the removal incomplete, so the evaluation fell back on cleaned subsets of the benchmarks with the overlapping items removed, GPT-3's second marker, in the Evaluation column ",e.jsx(s,{id:"brown2020language",children:"Brown et al., 2020"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["OLMo 3 documents its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decon"})," tool for two stages only, mid-training and long-context extension ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". OLMo 3 gives its reason: earlier results suggest that memorization is strongest near the end of training, so late data gets the strongest checks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". The two markers show where this recipe put its priority. They do not show that ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decon"})," never touched the main pretraining corpus."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{reading:!0,title:"Overlap checks trade recall for false positives",accent:6,number:"31",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Method"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Catches"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Misses or risks"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"n-gram overlap"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"near-verbatim spans"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"paraphrases, translations"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["sampled n-grams, cluster expansion (",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decon"}),")"]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"matches, cheaply at scale"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"semantic rewrites"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["embedding similarity (e.g., Nemotron 3 Super's cosine check ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron3super",children:"NVIDIA, 2026"}),")"]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"semantic neighbors"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"false positives at a threshold"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Broader matching catches more overlap and needs more review",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Every overlap check trades recall against compute and false positives. Recall is the share of overlapping items the check catches, and a false positive is clean text it removes by mistake."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["n-gram overlap compares short spans of consecutive tokens, the n-grams, between training documents and benchmark items. It is cheap and easy to interpret. A rewrite changes the spans, though: in the experiments of Yang et al., paraphrased and translated benchmark samples passed n-gram checks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"yang2023rephrased",children:"Yang et al., 2023"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["AllenAI's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decon"}),", the tool OLMo 3 runs, samples n-gram matches at corpus scale and then expands the matched clusters ",e.jsx(s,{id:"allenai2024decon",children:"AllenAI, 2024"}),". It still compares strings, so it inherits the n-gram check's blind spot for rewrites."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Embedding similarity compares learned representations instead of strings. Its risk is the threshold: one cutoff can remove legitimate text that merely discusses the same problem as a benchmark item. Nemotron 3 Super flags synthetic-code samples whose cosine similarity to items of HumanEval, MBPP, CRUXEval or LiveCodeBench exceeds 0.8 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron3super",children:"NVIDIA, 2026"}),". It reports this check only for a 0.2B-token slice of its synthetic code."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The broader the definition of a match, the more matches someone has to inspect. A decontamination plan therefore records four choices together: the comparison set, the representation, the cutoff and the inspection policy."})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{reading:!0,title:"Nemotron seeds QA from benchmark train splits",accent:7,number:"32",items:"2",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Nemotron: QA seeded from train splits ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2025nemotron3nano",children:"NVIDIA, 2025"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Decontaminated against test splits"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Two questions apply to benchmark-seeded data",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In Nemotron 3 Nano's synthetic question-answer (QA) data, the similarity to a benchmark is deliberate, because the seed items come from the benchmark's own task ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2025nemotron3nano",children:"NVIDIA, 2025"}),". Such similarity is harder to classify than the accidental overlap that n-gram and embedding checks look for."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Two separate questions apply to such data. Split hygiene asks whether a test item, or a near copy of one, reached the training data. Construct validity asks what capability a benchmark score is taken to measure at all. Week 7 developed the second question in "Every benchmark score serves a decision": the construct is the capability you care about, and a benchmark is one stand-in for it.'})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{reading:!0,title:"PII filtering is not decontamination",accent:0,number:"33",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["FineWeb: regex for emails, public IPs ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2024fineweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2024"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"olmOCR: is this document type public?"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Opt-outs, robots.txt, copyright: governance"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Privacy filtering and decontamination protect different things, and both need tests",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"PII filtering and decontamination both remove text from a corpus, but they protect different things. PII filtering protects people by keeping personal data, such as email addresses, out of the corpus. Decontamination protects a measurement by keeping benchmark items out, so that a score stays meaningful."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The two PII filters on the slide work at different levels. FineWeb's regular expressions edit strings inside a page ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2024fineweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2024"}),". olmOCR, OLMo 3's pipeline for academic PDFs, decides about a whole document. Its model-based filter asks whether the document is of a type intended for public dissemination ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". The secret and PII redaction in The Stack v3's code front end is the same lane for code. Polite crawling, robots.txt, opt-outs and copyright handling are governance. They decide what may be collected and kept, and none of them is evidence that benchmark items were removed. The legal side of data collection is outside this lecture."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["OLMo 3's report records a SQuAD and DROP preprocessing failure in its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decon"})," workflow ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". GPT-3's partial-removal bug is a second case. Both show why you test a decontamination pipeline against known positives, training text you know overlaps a benchmark. They also show why you inspect the cleaned evaluation subsets the pipeline produces."]})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Long-Context Data",children:[e.jsxs(a,{reading:!0,title:"Length alone does not buy long-range structure",accent:1,number:"34",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Order: pretrain, mid-train, extend context ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Packed short pages: nothing links across joins"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Natural long text: books, repositories, PDFs"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Packed short pages fill a window without long-range dependencies",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In OLMo 3 the extension comes last, after mid-training and its learning-rate decay ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". Other labs place it at other points. Week 11 covers mid-training composition in depth."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Extending the context involves two independent decisions. Position Interpolation and YaRN, from Week 3 and Bonus 2, change how positions are represented, so a model can operate beyond its original window. They do not choose the training sequences, which can be books, repositories, PDFs, replayed short text or synthetic tasks. That choice is a data decision."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Packing shows why it matters. A pipeline can fill a long window by concatenating short web pages until the sequence reaches the target length. Every prediction inside the sequence still resolves from nearby context, because the documents on either side of a join share no referent. A join between unrelated documents creates no dependency, so length alone teaches the model nothing about distance."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Natural long documents are rare and come from specific sources. Books preserve chapters and cross-references. Code repositories preserve dependencies across files, which is why the code front end groups files by repository. PDFs run about twice as long as web pages in FinePDFs, a large PDF corpus ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kydlicek2025finepdfs",children:"Kydlíček et al., 2025"}),". Document-boundary metadata therefore has to survive acquisition, or you cannot tell a long document from a long concatenation."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{reading:!0,title:"Replay, density and synthesis solve different failures",accent:2,number:"35",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["ProLong: short replay protects short tasks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gao2024prolong",children:"Gao et al., 2025"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Longmino: gzip filter for information density ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Longmino: ~16% synthetic aggregation tasks"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Each long-context mechanism answers a different failure",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Replay guards against regression. Extension shifts the training distribution toward long sequences, and short sequences mixed back in keep the short-context behavior the model already has. ProLong trains in two stages of 20B tokens each. The first uses sequences of 64K tokens, and the second sequences of 512K ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gao2024prolong",children:"Gao et al., 2025"}),". Its blend is roughly 60/40 long to short. Its short-task scores degrade monotonically as the long fraction grows. Nemotron 3 Nano reached the same lesson independently, at another scale. It found that 512K-token sequences alone hurt short benchmarks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2025nemotron3nano",children:"NVIDIA, 2025"}),". Its 121B-token extension therefore mixes them with 4K sequences."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compressibility filtering works on the long side itself, because a long document is not automatically informative. Longmino, the data mix of OLMo 3's long-context extension, blends long and short data at 34/66 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". It uses a gzip filter as a cheap proxy for information density. The filter works from both ends. It drops near-random text, which gzip can hardly shrink, and repetitive boilerplate, which it shrinks too much."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Synthetic tasks supervise the model, where the filter only selects data. Nothing in the next-token objective forces a model to use the long-range structure of natural documents, but a task whose answer requires combining passages that lie far apart does. About 16% of Longmino consists of such synthetic aggregation tasks. They come from one pool of long academic PDFs, which olmOCR, OLMo 3's PDF pipeline, turned into text. OLMo 2 Instruct then wrote the tasks over sections of each document ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". The tasks are of two types. Common word extraction (CWE) generates questions whose answer is the exact number of times one of five frequent single-word noun phrases occurs in a section. Rewriting expressions (REX) builds a task around one salient phrase and the passages that mention it, in formats such as a summary, a dialogue, flashcards or a quiz."]}),e.jsx(c,{title:"Longmino's two task types",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the 50B-token Longmino mix of the 7B model, CWE makes up 3.88% and REX 12.2%. Both start from olmOCR PDFs of 32K to 64K tokens ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),"."]})})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Synthesis",plate:"diderot-fer-blanc-atelier",children:[e.jsxs(a,{title:"Recipes disagree at three decisions",accent:4,number:"36",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Decision axis"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"FineWeb(-Edu)"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Nemotron-CC"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Dolma 3"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"MAI"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"keep/drop vs scored tiers"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"keep/drop"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"scored tiers"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"scored tiers"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"not documented"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"global removal vs later selective repetition"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"n/a: per-snapshot dedup"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"not documented"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"global, then selective repetition"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"not documented"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"natural-only vs synthetic augmentation"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"natural-only"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"synthetic augmentation"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"natural-only in pretraining"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"natural-only in pretraining"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Synthetic augmentation: an LLM rewrites real pages"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Four recipes share one skeleton and differ at three decisions",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:'FineWeb and FineWeb-Edu, Nemotron-CC, Dolma 3 and MAI build their corpora from the same skeleton of stages. They disagree at three decisions, each documented well enough to compare like for like. Each row records a choice. None ranks the recipes by the quality of their data. "Not documented" means the report does not describe that decision.'}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The first decision is what a quality score does. FineWeb-Edu applies one threshold and keeps or drops each document ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2024fineweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2024"}),". Nemotron-CC keeps the score as a tier, and its mixing stage samples the tiers at different rates ",e.jsx(s,{id:"su2024nemotroncc",children:"Su et al., 2025"}),". Dolma 3, OLMo 3's corpus, spends its score the same way: quality-aware upsampling repeats the better pages more often ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The second is the scope of deduplication. FineWeb removes duplicates inside each crawl snapshot ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2024fineweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2024"}),". Dolma 3 removes them across the whole corpus and then brings repetition back on purpose through the mixture ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),". The two give opposite answers to what a duplicate costs."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The third is whether rewritten text enters pretraining at all, which each recipe answers with yes or no. Nemotron-CC blends 1.9T synthetic tokens into its corpus ",e.jsx(s,{id:"su2024nemotroncc",children:"Su et al., 2025"}),". Dolma 3 keeps rephrased text out of its main pretraining mixture ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),", and so does MAI ",e.jsx(s,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",children:"Microsoft AI Team, 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The table leaves out PII filtering and decontamination, which stay two separate stages with separate purposes. A recipe can document one and say nothing about the other, and silence is no evidence that a step was skipped."})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"You filter, deduplicate, mix and compare",accent:5,number:"37",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Filters, then MinHash with exact verification"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Keep domain and provenance labels"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Report unique and sampled tokens, effective epochs"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Beat a provided unfiltered baseline, same budget"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Your shards keep their labels, and Week 13 runs on course-owned shards",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Your exercise reproduces the decisions that stay observable at small scale. You implement heuristic quality filters and MinHash with LSH yourself, verify every candidate pair with exact Jaccard similarity, and keep one document of each verified cluster by an explicit rule. Your output shards keep domain and provenance labels: the source revision, license, preprocessing, tokenizer, split and domain of each example. A provenance record in your submission describes the shards."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 13 asks whether a sparse model's experts specialize by domain, which needs examples labeled by domain. Its specialization pass reads course-owned canonical domain shards, with a smaller course-owned fallback when a provider or the cluster fails. Your own shards are at most an optional comparison there, and nothing in Week 13 depends on them."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The course trains the unfiltered baseline on the raw corpus. It uses the same model configuration, tokenizer and token budget as your curated run. Every run is scored on the same evaluation subset with Week 7's benchmark pipeline, and every run uses the same seeds. Besides the counts at every stage of your funnel and the verified duplicate clusters, you report each run's exposure accounting. It keeps a smaller curated corpus from receiving unreported extra epochs when it is compared with the unfiltered baseline. You also score your surviving corpus with the released FineWeb-Edu classifier, report what threshold 3 would keep, and show three removed documents per stage."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Matched conditions do not remove every confound. Your curated run and your run on a self-chosen domain mix draw from different source slices, so their difference changes provenance as well as mixture weights. Only a reference-mix result, a run with the natural weights over the same shards, isolates the weights. The course supplies its metrics next to the unfiltered-baseline checkpoint. Report the curated-against-mixed contrast as a change of both."})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Going further",children:[e.jsxs(a,{title:"Six more ideas from data pipelines",accent:6,number:"38",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Technique"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Problem it addresses"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["C4 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"raffel2020exploring",children:"Raffel et al., 2020"}),", RefinedWeb ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2023refinedweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2023"}),", Dolma ",e.jsx(s,{id:"soldaini2024dolma",children:"Soldaini et al., 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"from sources to open pipelines"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Optical character recognition (OCR) for PDFs ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kydlicek2025finepdfs",children:"Kydlíček et al., 2025"}),", per-language ID ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2025fineweb2",children:"Penedo et al., 2025"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"page images, per-language scores"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Code deduplication and filters ",e.jsx(s,{id:"lozhkov2026stackv3",children:"Lozhkov et al., 2026"}),", a majority-code mix ",e.jsx(s,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",children:"Microsoft AI Team, 2026"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"junk in source trees"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["phi-1's textbook data ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gunasekar2023textbooks",children:"Gunasekar et al., 2023"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"text with no source document"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Mixture schedules ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2025nemotron3nano",children:"NVIDIA, 2025"}),", mixture reuse ",e.jsx(s,{id:"chen2026olmix",children:"Chen et al., 2026"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"mixtures that change"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Long-context budgets ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"tokens for longer context"})]})]})]}),e.jsx(r,{title:"Six topics for readers who want to go further",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Pretraining corpora first came from a few chosen sources. Filtered web text such as C4 followed ",e.jsx(s,{id:"raffel2020exploring",children:"Raffel et al., 2020"}),". Then came whole pipelines that others can measure, such as Dolma ",e.jsx(s,{id:"soldaini2024dolma",children:"Soldaini et al., 2024"}),". Some scientific PDFs hold their text only as page images, so they need optical character recognition. A language classifier's scores also need calibrating for each language ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2025fineweb2",children:"Penedo et al., 2025"}),". Code runs this lecture's deduplication with settings and filters of its own ",e.jsx(s,{id:"lozhkov2026stackv3",children:"Lozhkov et al., 2026"}),". One 2026 mix draws most of its tokens from code ",e.jsx(s,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",children:"Microsoft AI Team, 2026"}),". phi-1 generated textbook-style training text with no source document behind it ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gunasekar2023textbooks",children:"Gunasekar et al., 2023"}),". A mixture can also change during training ",e.jsx(s,{id:"nvidia2025nemotron3nano",children:"NVIDIA, 2025"}),". When the domain set changes during development, part of the previous mixture can be reused ",e.jsx(s,{id:"chen2026olmix",children:"Chen et al., 2026"}),". Open models spend between 5B and 840B tokens on extending their context ",e.jsx(s,{id:"ettinger2025olmo3",children:"Ettinger et al., 2025"}),"."]})})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Pretraining corpora went from sources to pipelines",optional:!0,accent:4,number:"39",children:[e.jsx(w,{}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Curated sources, then filtered web"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Now: open, measured pipelines"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The unit of corpus engineering grew from sources to pipelines",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The timeline sorts well-known pretraining corpora into three eras. What changes from era to era is what gets engineered: first the choice of sources, then the filtering of the open web, and now the whole pipeline, published so that others can inspect and measure it."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The first corpora were a few selected sources. BERT was pretrained on English Wikipedia and BooksCorpus ",e.jsx(s,{id:"devlin2019bert",children:"Devlin et al., 2019"}),". GPT-2 introduced WebText ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gpt2-2019",children:"Radford et al., 2019"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The second era trained on the open web at scale, which required filtering. C4 made large-scale web extraction and its rules visible ",e.jsx(s,{id:"raffel2020exploring",children:"Raffel et al., 2020"}),". The Pile emphasized diversity of sources ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gao2020pile",children:"Gao et al., 2020"}),". MassiveText documented a large rule-based quality pipeline ",e.jsx(s,{id:"rae2021gopher",children:"Rae et al., 2021"}),". RefinedWeb showed that a carefully processed web corpus could compete with curated mixtures ",e.jsx(s,{id:"penedo2023refinedweb",children:"Penedo et al., 2023"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the third era the pipeline itself is released. Dolma published both a multi-source corpus and the tooling used to build it ",e.jsx(s,{id:"soldaini2024dolma",children:"Soldaini et al., 2024"}),". FineWeb and Nemotron-CC continue the shift toward measured, reproducible decisions. CS336 lectures 13 and 14 give the longer history and the legal context ",e.jsx(s,{id:"cs336-2026",children:"Hashimoto & Liang, 2026"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"PDFs and other languages need their own extraction",optional:!0,accent:6,number:"40",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Optical character recognition (OCR) for science PDFs"}),`
